## What a `type: link` instance is

A first-class representation of a MARKDOWN LINK extracted from a memorial body — `[anchor-text](target-rel-path)` becomes a `:mm/Link` entity capturing the href, anchor text, containing section, external-vs-internal flag, and (for internal links) the target memorial reference.  Links are not authored directly; they're produced by the codec's body-content parsing.

## Why links are first-class

Treating links as entities (rather than just substrings in body content) enables:

- **Backlink queries** — "what memorials link TO this one?" via inbound walk on `:mm.link/target-memory`
- **Link-shape audits** — broken-link detection becomes a substrate invariant
- **Section-precise origin** — each link knows which `:mm/Section` of its memorial contained it (via `:mm.link/in-section`), enabling section-level "what other memorials does this section link to?" queries
- **External-vs-internal classification** — `:mm.link/external?` distinguishes corpus-internal refs from web URLs

